Regular expressions with Python's `re` syntax and API, matched in time linear in the text.

| Type | What it is | Python |
|---|---|---|
| `ca::regex` (`basic_regex<A>`) | a compiled pattern, in memory from A | `re.Pattern` |
| `ca::regex_pattern` | a pattern's text: checked when it compiles if literal | the pattern string |
| `ca::regex_flags` | `ignore_case`, `multiline`, `dot_all`, `verbose`, `ascii` | `re.I`, `re.M`, `re.S`, `re.X`, `re.A` |
| `ca::regex_match<A>` | one match: groups, positions, `expand` | `re.Match` |
| `regex_matches<R>` (`R::matches_type`) | the matches one by one, from `finditer` | the iterator of `finditer` |
| `ca::regex_error` | why a pattern does not compile, and where | `re.error` (`msg`, `pos`) |

`ca::regex::make(pattern, flags)` (or `compile`) acquires a regex: `result<regex>`, failing with
`errc::invalid_argument` and the syntax error's message as the trace frame, or out of memory. A
literal pattern is checked when the program compiles, like a format string: `make("a)b")` is a
compile error naming `regex_has_unbalanced_paren`. Text known only at runtime goes in explicitly,
`ca::regex_pattern{text}`, and `regex_pattern{text}.check(flags)` gives the error's kind and
position without building anything (constexpr).

The methods are Python's: `search`, `match`, `fullmatch` (`result<optional<regex_match>>`, the
result failing only out of memory), `finditer`, `findall`, `split`, `sub` (a template with `\1`,
`\g<name>`, or a function of the match), `escape`, `group_count`, `group_index`. A match borrows
the text and the regex's group names; `m[1]` traps on a group the pattern does not have and is
empty for one that took no part, `m.group(1)`, `m.start(1)`, `m.end(1)` give nothing then.
Positions count code units (bytes of UTF-8), as everywhere in ca; `search(text, pos)` starts at
pos while `^` and `\b` still see what precedes it, and a prefix of the text plays Python's endpos.

```c++
#include "ca/regex.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto date = ca::regex::make("(?P<year>\\d{4})-(?P<month>\\d\\d)");
	if (!date)
		return 1;
	auto found = date->search("paid 2026-11, due 2026-12");
	if (!found || !*found)
		return 2;
	ca::regex_match<>& m = **found;
	if (m.group() != ca::sv::str{"2026-11"} || m["month"] != ca::sv::str{"11"} || m.start() != 5U)
		return 3;
	auto swapped = date->sub("\\g<month>/\\g<year>", "2026-11 and 2026-12");
	if (!swapped || *swapped != ca::sv::str{"11/2026 and 12/2026"})
		return 4;
	auto words = ca::regex::make("\\W+");
	if (!words)
		return 5;
	auto pieces = words->split("one, two;three");
	if (!pieces || pieces->size() != 3U || (*pieces)[2U] != ca::sv::str{"three"})
		return 6;
	return m.cleanup() && swapped->cleanup() && pieces->cleanup() && words->cleanup() && date->cleanup() ? 0 : 7;
}
```

### Syntax

Python's, over UTF-8: literals and escapes (`\n \t \xhh \uhhhh \Uhhhhhhhh`, octal `\0` and
`\ooo`), `.`, classes `[a-z]`, `[^...]` with `\d \w \s` and their negations inside, anchors
`^ $ \A \Z \b \B`, groups `(...)`, `(?:...)`, `(?P<name>...)`, comments `(?#...)`, alternation,
the greedy and lazy repeats `* + ? {n} {n,} {,m} {n,m}`, inline flags `(?imsxa)` at the start and
`(?i:...)`, `(?-i:...)` for a group. Unknown escapes of ASCII letters are errors, as in Python.

`\w \d \s` are Unicode's (Python's `str.isalnum` or `_`, `str.isdecimal`, `str.isspace`), ASCII
under `ascii`; `ignore_case` takes characters as Python's re does (same simple lowercase, plus
re's extra pairs: `k`, `K` and the Kelvin sign; `s`, `S` and `ſ`; ...). The classes and the
caseless pairs are tables generated from the running Python and checked against its re for every
code point (`make unicode`). `\B` matches an empty text, as in Python 3.14 (not 3.12).

### What a linear-time engine leaves out

Backreferences (`\1` in a pattern, `(?P=name)`), lookahead and lookbehind, conditionals `(?(1)...)`,
atomic groups and possessive repeats, `\N{name}`: each is an error of its own kind
(`regex_error::kind::backreference`, ...), at compile time for a literal. In exchange no pattern
backtracks: matching costs O(text size * program size) whatever the input, with memory fixed when
the regex is made. Patterns are bounded (groups nested 128 deep, repeats up to 100000, a program of
2^18 instructions and transitions), so a regex cannot take unbounded memory either.

### Matching

The pattern compiles to a Thompson NFA over UTF-8 bytes (a character class becomes a trie of byte
ranges), run as a Pike VM: every possible thread advances one byte at a time, in priority order,
so the first thread to match is the match Python's backtracker would find (leftmost-first, greedy
and lazy repeats as written), groups included. A loop whose body can match empty iterates as
Python's does: past its minimum, an iteration that matched empty ends it, keeping its groups.
Before running, the matcher skips the text to where a match can begin: the literal every match
starts with (a substring search), else the bytes a match can start with. A pattern that is only a
literal never runs the VM.

The scratch memory of the VM is taken by `make`, so matching allocates only what it returns (a
match's groups when the pattern has groups, the lists of findall and split, sub's string). The
matching methods therefore change the regex: one thread uses it at a time, `clone()` makes one
for another thread. Text that is not valid UTF-8 can be searched: invalid bytes match no
character, `.` and negated classes included.

```c++
#include "ca/regex.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto vowels = ca::regex::make("[aeiouy]+", ca::regex_flags::ignore_case);
	if (!vowels)
		return 1;
	auto all = vowels->findall("SEA Breeze");
	if (!all || all->size() != 3U || (*all)[0U] != ca::sv::str{"EA"})
		return 2;
	auto matches = vowels->finditer("Kepler");
	ca::usz count = 0U;
	while (true) {
		auto next = matches.next();
		if (!next)
			return 3;
		if (!*next)
			break;
		++count;
		if (!(*next)->cleanup())
			return 4;
	}
	const ca::optional<ca::regex_error> problem = ca::regex_pattern{ca::sv::str{"(a)\\1"}}.check();
	if (count != 2U || !problem || problem->what() != ca::regex_error::kind::backreference)
		return 5;
	return all->cleanup() && vowels->cleanup() ? 0 : 6;
}
```
